		{"id":"b899d8fd05e60755","type":"text","text":"[Tone]\nProfessional, educational, engaging, practical.\n\nShow don't tell.","x":-620,"y":-194,"width":250,"height":220},
		{"id":"e4ce0f2c27c7ebf7","type":"text","text":"[skills]\n\n1. **Module 1: Introduction to Physical Therapy**\n    \n    - Proficiency in understanding the role and impact of a physical therapy aide within a healthcare team.\n    - Knowledge of career opportunities and growth paths in physical therapy.\n    - Application of ethical principles and professional conduct in healthcare settings.\n2. **Module 2: Anatomy and Physiology**\n    \n    - Identification of human anatomical structures and understanding their functions.\n    - Basic knowledge of muscle and skeletal systems relevant to physical therapy.\n    - Ability to relate common conditions to anatomical knowledge for practical application.\n3. **Module 3: Medical Terminology**\n    \n    - Mastery of medical terminology specific to physical therapy.\n    - Skill in accurately describing body parts, symptoms, and treatments.\n4. **Module 4: Clinical Procedures**\n    \n    - Competence in preparing and maintaining clinical treatment areas.\n    - Skills in safe patient transfer and positioning.\n    - Adherence to infection control and hygiene standards.\n5. **Module 5: Patient Communication and Care**\n    \n    - Development of effective communication strategies for patient care.\n    - Techniques for ensuring patient comfort and providing assistance.\n    - Understanding and application of patient rights and confidentiality.\n6. **Module 6: Supportive Modalities**\n    \n    - Proficiency in using and maintaining physical therapy equipment.\n    - Assisting with therapeutic exercises under professional supervision.\n    - Basic first aid and CPR skills (subject to certification requirements).\n7. **Module 7: Workplace Skills**\n    \n    - Ability to perform administrative tasks in a physical therapy setting.\n    - Management of scheduling and patient flow.\n    - Basic understanding of health insurance and billing processes.\n8. **Module 8: Practical Experience**\n    \n    - Hands-on experience in clinical procedures and patient interaction.\n    - Comprehensive understanding of physical therapy clinic workflow.\n    - Building confidence in a professional healthcare environment.\n9. **Module 9: Job Readiness and Professional Development**\n    \n    - Resume writing and interview skills for physical therapy jobs.\n    - Networking and job searching techniques in healthcare.\n    - Awareness of continuing education and career advancement opportunities.\n","x":895,"y":-823,"width":810,"height":1783},
		{"id":"6b08fd22e19786b1","type":"text","text":"{{[[Course Builder V1/Project Creator]]}} create a project for {{outline}}","x":-3920,"y":4363,"width":250,"height":197,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"e206a944b2d106de","type":"text","text":"## Module 1: Introduction to Physical Therapy\n\n### 1.1 Understanding the Role of Physical Therapy Aides\n- Rationale: Grasping the importance of assistive roles in healthcare for efficient functioning and patient satisfaction.\n- Expectations: Learners will be able to describe the day-to-day responsibilities and the value of a physical therapy aide in a clinical setting.\n- Pre-Assessment: A series of scenarios in which the learner identifies appropriate aide interventions to facilitate physical therapy processes.\n\n### 1.2 Exploring Career Paths in Physical Therapy\n- Rationale: Providing insights into the diverse career options within the physical therapy field to set realistic goals and expectations.\n- Expectations: Learners will be able to list and differentiate various career roles and the educational paths required for each.\n- Pre-Assessment: A questionnaire that assesses the learner’s career interests and understanding of each role’s scope and duties.\n\n### 1.3 Ethical Conduct and Professionalism\n- Rationale: Establishing the behavioral standards and ethical considerations necessary for trust and integrity in healthcare professions.\n- Expectations: Learners will recognize and apply ethical principles and professional behaviors in hypothetical healthcare scenarios.\n- Pre-Assessment: Ethics-based situational judgment questions that gauge learners' initial understanding of professional conduct and ethics in physical therapy.\n\n### 1.4 Core Concepts in Physical Therapy Aides' Functions\n- Concepts and Theories: Comprehend the basic theories of physical therapy and how aides support therapists and patients.\n- Walkthrough: Detailed process of a PT aide's role in patient reception, preparation of treatment areas, and equipment handling.\n- Best Practices: Emphasizing the importance of a clean and organized workspace, patient confidentiality, and clear communication with the PT team.\n\n### 1.5 Effective Interaction with the Healthcare Team\n- Concepts and Theories: Explore interprofessional collaboration and the multidisciplinary approach in patient care.\n- Walkthrough: Step-by-step guide on how to interact with physical therapists, PT assistants, and other healthcare professionals.\n- Best Practices: Techniques in active listening, assertive communication, and supporting team decisions.\n\n### 1.6 Advancing in the Physical Therapy Career\n- Concepts and Theories: Overview of continuous learning, certification opportunities, and specialization areas.\n- Walkthrough: Pathways to becoming a PT, roles in management, academia, and other specialized sectors of physical therapy.\n- Best Practices: Strategies for professional growth, including pursuing advanced certifications and leveraging professional networks.\n\n### 1.7 Case Study: The Effective Physical Therapy Aide\n- Case Study: A fictional scenario where a physical therapy aide exemplifies exceptional service, exhibits professionalism, and supports patient care.\n- Detailed narration of the aide's interactions with patients and the healthcare team, showcasing best practices in action.\n\n### 1.8 Application: The Role Play of Clinic Scenarios\n- Practice Activity: Simulated exercises where learners assume the role of a PT aide in various clinical situations, enabling them to practice and reinforce skills learned.\n- Scenarios include prepping treatment areas, engaging with simulated patients, and collaborating with a virtual healthcare team.","x":120,"y":1880,"width":1640,"height":1600,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"6d4cb37e40c281eb","type":"text","text":"[audience]\nAdult learners looking to upskill or for a career change\n\nLittle or no experience with physical therapy aide terminology and practices. ","x":-340,"y":-194,"width":250,"height":220},
		{"id":"18795c84f0d61402","type":"text","text":"### 1.1 Understanding the Role of Physical Therapy Aides\n\n#### Rationale: Grasping the importance of assistive roles in healthcare for efficient functioning and patient satisfaction.\n\nIn the bustling environment of a healthcare facility, each role carries a significant weight in the orchestra of patient care. Physical Therapy Aides serve not merely as cogs in this machine but as essential elements elevating the operability of the physical therapy unit. Your understanding of this role not only lays the groundwork for operational efficiency but also amplifies patient satisfaction – a paramount endgame in healthcare services.\n\nPicture yourself in a crowded physical therapy clinic where patients require immediate care – it is the Physical Therapy Aide who steps in to streamline the process. Your swift actions in preparing the treatment space, sanitizing equipment, and providing the necessary support to therapists and patients ensure that the delivery of services is not hampered by logistical setbacks.\n\nThe significance of this role transcends menial tasks; it encapsulates the essence of teamwork and the overarching goal of enhancing patient experiences. As you partake in this course, you equip yourself to become an indispensable asset to any physical therapy team, improving patient outcomes and contributing to a more effective health system.\n\nIn essence, this knowledge and subsequent practical mastery will forge your capability to act with confidence, anticipate the needs within the practice, and act proactively – attributes that hold immediate relevance not only to your workplace but also to the broader scope of your professional and personal development.\n\nIn workplaces that are increasingly valuing the ability to work autonomously, and where health-sector roles are highly dynamic, the competency of a Physical Therapy Aide becomes indispensable. As such, learning about this role is not just about acquiring skills; it's about understanding your place in the greater healthcare ecosystem and the impact you can effect through your service.","x":-2600,"y":1880,"width":888,"height":1147,"color":"6"},
